Two questions answered on the merits, both handed back to me.

THE ROOT AND STAKE IN FLIGHT. The option chosen blind was to credit open stake
as cr and void the shots. It was never applied — LEGACY_ROOT_LIVE.cmd passes no
--allow-open-stake, so the run that printed ROOT BUILT (17 leaves, depth 5) had
genuinely zero open stake under the EXISTING rule. Which is lucky, because that
option is wrong twice over: it hands a player who sealed a CORRECT prediction
their stake back instead of their win, selected by nothing but holding a chamber
at the cutover second; and the void has to land in the store atomically with the
snapshot, so a half-applied one leaves a wallet credited on chain and still
holding the shot off chain, paid twice, on the one path that cannot be rolled
back.

So: freeze, drain, snapshot. RX_MIGRATION_FREEZE=1 refuses in takeStake, the
only place credits are ever COMMITTED — every shot, every challenge, every take.
Settlement, reveals, claims and the crank are untouched: it stops selling, never
settling. Refusals carry MIGRATION_FREEZE rather than a generic SHOT_REFUSED,
because an agent that gets an opaque refusal for a day cannot tell a freeze from
a fault, which was the entire shape of the Bankr RELEASE_MISMATCH failure. An
env var and not a store key: the cutover is one announced act, and a switch that
ships in the source is one anybody can verify against the running release.

The objection that "refuse while open" cannot scale to a thousand players is
true and irrelevant. The legacy root is set ONCE; after cutover the crossing
predicate settles balances on chain and there is no snapshot any more. The rule
must survive one cutover, not a thousand players.

And the second guard was dead. set-legacy-root.mjs refuses to patch LEGACY_ROOT
when any row of merkle_balances.json carries staked > 0 — an independent check
that fires exactly when the builder was run with --allow-open-stake. The builder
wrote a hardcoded `staked: 0` for everybody, so it read a constant and passed. A
check that cannot fail is worse than no check: the money path looked doubly
protected. It now carries the real per-wallet stake.

STOCKS. The keyless path is already proven — the tokenized xStocks are filed
under Crypto, Pyth sponsors those on Solana, and the frozen program can read
them with no change. What blocks them is one number against another: an 870s
batch cadence against a seal bound that clamps at 60s, so ~7 seals in 100 land.

The recorded option was to scale the seal bound to the horizon. That trades a
refusal problem for an information-asymmetry problem: a player sealing on a
15-minute-old price knows which way TSLAX moved and the program does not. The
existing defence — "a rounding error on a day-long call" — is a comfort
argument, not a bound, and asymmetries get harvested patiently.

The fourth option, available only since ruleset 2 shipped HORIZON_MASK: bind the
ENTRY crossing forward. entry = the unique publish where prev_publish_time <
sealed_ts <= publish_time — the same predicate bind_crossing already binds at
expiry, pointed at the other end of the shot. You cannot seal on a stale price
if your entry does not exist yet. Seals go from 7% to ~100% with no promise
relaxed; the promise gets stronger, from "your entry is recent" to "your entry
is unknowable at seal". It composes with the mask rather than replacing it:
binding makes the entry honest, the mask keeps the window long enough that the
binding delay is noise. Per-feed, because on SOL's 60s heartbeat it would eat
20% of a 5-minute window for no benefit. Ruleset 3 work, written down, not built.

The measurement it rests on could not be reproduced from this repository: the
870s came from ids fetched ad hoc on 2026-09-02 and never committed, by a script
that was not kept. docs/STOCK_FEEDS.json pins all eight feeds and their
redemption-rate siblings; tools/stock_cadence.mjs measures properly, with SOL as
a control that refuses to let a lying RPC produce a confident wrong answer, and
prints the two decision numbers directly — pass rate per horizon today, and the
forward-binding delay as a fraction of each window. Its restatement of the
horizon table and max_seal_age is pinned to lib.rs by test, so a program change
fails the tool instead of quietly making its report describe a different game.

npm test: 109 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped — the full gate this time, not
run-tests.mjs. check-versions caught the runner digests, kill-switch caught an
undocumented env var. Skill 1.6.3.

